    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p39-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that the number of selfish subsets of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}$ of cardinality $k$ is $\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{n-1}{k-1}$.\n\nInclude $k$, then choose the other $k-1$ elements from the remaining $n-1$ elements.",
          "worked": "A subset of cardinality $k$ is selfish exactly when it contains $k$. After including $k$, choose its remaining $k-1$ elements from the other $n-1$ elements. Thus the number is\n$$\\binom{n-1}{k-1}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that the number of selfish subsets of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}$ is $2^{n-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2^{n-1}$.\n\nSum the counts in part (a) and use the binomial theorem.",
          "worked": "Let $S(n)$ be the total number of selfish subsets. Summing over their possible cardinalities gives\n$$\\begin{align*}S(n)&=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\binom{n-1}{k-1}\\\\S(n)&=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\binom{n-1}{j}\\\\S(n)&=(1+1)^{n-1}=2^{n-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe last equality uses the binomial theorem.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that the number of minimal selfish subsets of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}$ of cardinality $k$ is $\\binom{n-k}{k-1}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{n-k}{k-1}$.\n\nA selfish set of cardinality $k$ is minimal exactly when its least element is $k$.",
          "worked": "Let $A$ be selfish with $|A|=k$, so $k\\in A$. If $A$ contains an element $j<k$, choose $j-1$ other elements of $A$. Together with $j$, they form a selfish proper subset of cardinality $j$. Thus minimality requires $\\min A=k$.\n\nConversely, if $\\min A=k$, every proper subset $B$ has $|B|<k$, while every element of $B$ is at least $k$. Hence $|B|\\notin B$, so $B$ is not selfish. Therefore $A$ is minimal.\n\nInclude $k$ and choose the other $k-1$ elements from $\\{k+1,\\ldots,n\\}$, which has $n-k$ elements. The number is\n$$\\binom{n-k}{k-1},$$\nwith zero choices when $k-1>n-k$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that the number of minimal selfish subsets of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}$ is equal to the $n$th Fibonacci number.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$F_n$, where $F_1=F_2=1$.\n\nThe counts have initial values $1,1$ and satisfy the Fibonacci recurrence.",
          "worked": "Let $M(n)$ be the total number of minimal selfish subsets. Part (c), with $j=k-1$, gives\n$$M(n)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\lfloor(n-1)/2\\rfloor}\\binom{n-1-j}{j}.$$\nFor $n=1$ and $n=2$, the only minimal selfish subset is $\\{1\\}$, so $M(1)=M(2)=1$.\n\nFor $n\\ge3$, use Pascal's identity. Interpret $\\binom{a}{b}$ as zero when $b<0$ or $b>a$:\n$$\\begin{align*}M(n)&=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\lfloor(n-1)/2\\rfloor}\\left[\\binom{n-2-j}{j}+\\binom{n-2-j}{j-1}\\right]\\\\M(n)&=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\lfloor(n-2)/2\\rfloor}\\binom{n-2-j}{j}+\\sum_{j=1}^{\\lfloor(n-1)/2\\rfloor}\\binom{n-2-j}{j-1}\\\\M(n)&=M(n-1)+\\sum_{r=0}^{\\lfloor(n-3)/2\\rfloor}\\binom{n-3-r}{r}\\\\M(n)&=M(n-1)+M(n-2).\\end{align*}$$\nThese are the Fibonacci initial values and recurrence. Hence $M(n)=F_n$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      }
    ]
  },
